# The Distinctive Character of the Tamils
Tamil title: தமிழரின் தனிப் பண்பு

This speech was delivered by Arignar Anna under the chairmanship of Mr Sithaiyan at the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam conference held at Alagiri Thidal, opposite the Salt Cotaurs in Madras, on Saturday, 21 January 1950.

I am satisfied by the feeling visible among the people during these two days. Some comrades become angry that admission is charged for the conference. We must conduct both the conference and the Kazhagam with the public's money. To run the organisation, we cannot carry the *kavadi* before the Birlas of the South.

It is better to spend money from a poor person's torn pocket than two thousand rupees from a rich man's pocket; the former possesses greater power. You know that nine people live in a leaking hut. Since this movement is conducted by poor people, it is the poor who must give it financial support.

## We Lost Many Opportunities

Many opportunities arose to determine this country's future political condition, and we allowed them all to slip away.

For example, Sir Stafford Cripps came intending to learn the views of the subcontinent's various political leaders and find a path. Only after he had met every leader, reached a conclusion and closed his diary did our leader meet him. That meeting produced no benefit.

Our leader recently wrote in a newspaper that I refused to accompany him when he wished to meet comrade Jinnah and that I betrayed him then. It is true that I refused to go. I said, “Meet Jinnah with a definite conclusion and programme in hand; there is no benefit in meeting him merely as a courtesy.” I refused because I understood that the leader's meeting would be only ceremonial.

The editor of the *Sunday Observer*, comrade P. Balasubramaniam, and comrade K. M. Balasubramaniam, who now lives as a devout Saivite, accompanied the leader. All three returned disappointed. The opportunity was missed then too. The chance to meet comrade Ambedkar also proved fruitless. Countless opportunities were lost in this manner.

## Heroes Only in Speech

We are not fools who say we shall do this and that and then do nothing. When we say something, we desire to demonstrate it in practice under peaceful conditions.

Congress people once spoke heroically. Later they bowed before everything to which they had declared they would never bow.

In 1935, comrade S. Satyamurti roared like a lion: “We shall break the Act and create a new law.” What happened?

They broke no law. Ministers administer through that very same Act.

I place the country's vital problems before you. Could we not cunningly derail a moving train? Is cutting telegraph wires impossible? A hammer, a small penknife, a little courage in the heart, and arranging for the police to suffer a little forgetfulness would be enough. But how can violence elevate the country's condition?

## Our Freedom Is a Pigeon on a Tower

We must catch a pigeon perched atop a tower. The man who must catch it has one disabled leg. The pigeon is ours, but the tower belongs to another. The man who must climb it has lost the use of one leg. With that disabled leg, we must catch the pigeon and display it to Dravidian society. This is the problem before us.

While we speak here between lion and tiger, Vijayalakshmi, Nehru's beloved sister and a woman of leisure, may be speaking pleasantly in Washington as India's ambassador. In Russia, the ambassador is a Vedantist. There is an ambassador in France, and they possess many ambassadors in other countries.

The flag bearing the Ashoka Chakra flies in the sky; the tricolour flies upon the ocean. Their flag flies at Government institutions and gracefully even in foreign countries. We have not forgotten this condition. It is a force of that magnitude that we must oppose.

Members of the DMK, if the Government sends an invitation asking, “Will you attend the Republic Day celebration?”, we shall certainly refuse it.

Now that the public understands everything, we demand that the constitutional scheme be altered.

## We Shall Change the Constitution

I place inexhaustible confidence in you. I believe that with the support of your strength we can accomplish many things.

Under the Ashoka Chakra flag flying over Fort St George as though trying to touch the sky, we can alter the Constitution. To amend it we require a two-thirds majority. We are not going to contest the election, but many among the independent parties' supporters will be our people.

The district board election has taught Congress a good lesson. In the Assembly, two hundred people wait to raise their hands the instant they are told, “Raise your hand.” How long can this continue?

Those who understand political forecasting can see clearly what the future will bring. Can democracy fail to blossom in the Legislature? It will soon blossom there, and we shall witness it.

More than half the batter in the pot goes north. One cannot make *dosai* with what little remains. The honourable Gopal Reddiar must understand this well.

## We Shall Form a United Front

We shall certainly amend the Constitution and establish a united front in the Indian subcontinent.

Assam produces abundant tea; only with it can the province conduct its Government. An Assam representative has warned, “On the basis of our old entitlement, we might even join Burma.” You may have read in the newspapers Feroz Khan Noon's statement that Pakistan might become part of the Soviet sphere.

Why can we not obtain a majority by joining Assam, resource-rich Bengal, Andhra renowned for its anger, Kerala in alarm, and incomparable Tamil Nadu?

In this gathering I see young people who are the spears at the battlefront. The cigarettes held in their hands appear to me as sharp guns. I see most of them in military dress, and in my mind's eye many stand bearing the beautiful Dravidian flag.

Bharati sang, “Behold the mother's jewelled flag; come bow and praise it,” before the mother's flag flew over the Fort. Namakkal Kavignar sang, “A war is coming without sword or blood,” before the war began.

## The Distinctive Character of the Tamils

Because I know the finest quality of the Tamils well, I am certain that we shall see Dravida Nadu within our lifetime. You too must possess that confidence.

Let us sound in every direction the heroic declaration that 26 January is a day of dissatisfaction. It is not a day of mourning; it is a day upon which we prepare ourselves.

The fox has its thicket, the tiger its cave, the snake its anthill and birds their branches. Should there not be a place where all Dravidians can live together? Can we wander seeking some lord residing in glorious Brahmapuri for this purpose? Are we incapable of governing a country, or have we never done so?

At no time did northerners dominate from the Vindhyas to Kanyakumari—not in Chandragupta's time, Kanishka's time or Pulakesin's. Even Aurangzeb could not cross south of the Vindhyas. Whatever they ruled lay beyond those mountains. Invasions occurred, and they were repelled.

You know the ten avatars. Only two took human form, and even those two appeared north of the Vindhyas.

As said in the play, Tamils know only two things: friendship and enmity. They know nothing between them. But Tamils have made northerners who attacked Tamil Nadu submit.

We point out that the Dravidian people's religious life is bound to Aryans, its politics to Delhi and its economy to Bombay, and we demand a separate Government for four and a half crore people. What is wrong in this?

Europe contains France, Spain, Portugal, Latvia, Lithuania, Greece, Albania and Italy, each a separate country. Dravida Nadu is larger in area than many of them. It possesses fertile land, water, mountains and human wealth.

How many places of natural beauty lie unattended in prosperous Dravida Nadu. We see winding canals, fields beside them, small plots where farmers sing with joyful hearts, roads beside those fields, groves beside the roads where parrots coo, and red tender coconuts on the other side of the grove.

There are hills by the roads, pearls in the sea, fragrant sandalwood and musk varieties in the forest, beautiful timber and many other resources. The honey of delight flows through pleasant Dravidam.

France may possess coal but lack petroleum; England may possess coal but lack gold; Germany may possess iron but lack uranium. No separate European country possesses resources as we do here.

## Mineral Exploration

Below our soil lie gold and coal. More than that, Travancore contains uranium essential for producing atomic bombs in quantities that could make America fear us. If we dig intelligently, every rare resource can be found. We are too lazy to dig. Excavation would produce immense benefit, but the Government pays no attention to exploring for needed resources.

There is a Department of Archaeology. Do you know what its people excavated and discovered? Panchamukhalingam, a Nataraja dancing with his left leg raised, and a Pillaiyar with his consort. Such are their discoveries. Comrade Ramachandra Dikshitar heads it. Is this a path to national welfare?

A thought from the *Kural* now comes to mind: the maiden called Earth laughs when she sees a man who possesses land yet lies in hunger.

“When she sees those who plead poverty and remain idle,
the good woman called Earth laughs.” (*Kural* 1040)

O man! Madman who drives a nail made by the Tata company, hangs a picture of Saraswati printed in Germany, burns camphor sent by Japan and admires the display! Tamil who looks abroad for everything even while natural wealth abounds here and all things can be obtained here—can you govern a country? The maiden Earth looks at the Tamil and laughs.

## If the Hand Is Lost, the Eye Remains

Northerners may have established domination in Tamil Nadu through deceit and intrigue. Before the sword-bearing Tamil alone must they submit.

Until they cut off our thumbs, we shall continue to write. Until they sever the tips of our tongues, we shall continue to speak. Even if they cut off thumb and tongue, the eyes will speak more beautifully than the mouth. Will they fail to speak?

## We Ask Nehru

A beggar walks while another person travels in a gleaming motor car. If you turn your gaze upon the beggar, do not his eyes speak eloquently of his poverty?

We ask that facilities be provided according to our objective. Whom do we ask? We ask Nehru, who knows world history and geography.

## We Are Warriors

Did Nehru's Government not help when China faced danger? Did we not observe China Day and Spain Day? Who were the heroes who shed blood in the Kashmir struggle? Were they not predominantly Dravidian youth?

Tamil Nadu's warriors stand at the front and give their lives whenever war comes. Tamil warriors stood in the front during the Home Rule struggle. When wars occurred on the banks of the Nile, in Egypt, France and Germany, Tamil Nadu's soldiers shed their blood.

If we wished, could we not form a great army? Could we not gather soldiers who have travelled abroad and returned and create a force? Why should it be impossible?

## Let Us Build Ships and Live

If we wished, could we not establish in our country an I.N.O. like the U.N.O.?

When a great wind blows, the fine trees of the forest seem to ask us, “Why do you keep us forever as trees? Please use us to build beautiful ships so that we may float happily upon the surging sea.” If we wished, could we not build beautiful ships?

Need we seek evidence in *Kalingattuparani* for our valour? It describes the heroic work of the Tamil clearly. To conquer difficult terrain, it says elephants were sent. Neither Vedas nor Vedic priests were sent.

When jewelled crowns rested upon the heads of Tamil kings, Dravida Nadu did not fade.

## A Wall for a House, a Fortification for a Country

Each house has a wall and each river its dam. Is that not a form of division? When every house needs a wall, what is wrong if every country has a boundary?

Even when a door exists between neighbouring houses, if the neighbour begins a conversation late at night as we go to bed, do we not say, “Let us discuss everything tomorrow morning,” and bolt the door?

Does every field not possess its own bund? From an aeroplane, all the fields below may resemble a single green silk cloth. Can we therefore say, “They all look like one field to us; anyone who wishes may harvest them”?

Because the play *Chandramohan* is being staged tonight and I must speak in it not merely about political authority but also social questions, I conclude my address here.

---NOTES---
The OCR date “21-71-1950” is invalid. The printed weekday is Saturday, and 21 January 1950 was a Saturday; the translation follows that reading while preserving the source.

The mixed-script abbreviation on image 8 clearly reads “I.N.O. - U.N.O.” in the scan. The source does not expand I.N.O.; it is retained without inventing a name.

The source uses period political and regional generalisations. They are translated as historical rhetoric, not presented as contemporary ethnographic claims.

The Archaeology Department passage appears to use “புதைபொருள் ஆராய்ச்சி” broadly while criticising an emphasis on religious antiquities rather than mineral exploration; the contrast is preserved.
